Pressing Ctrl+\ (SIGQUIT) during a piped yoyo -p ... | tee log run now writes out the text produced so far and exits 131. Before, everything was lost. Committed as 77f17d71, with the build, all tests, clippy, fmt and harness_logic.sh passing.

Before the fix: I wrote the new exact-bytes test first and ran it on the old code. Stdout was empty and the process died by signal, with no exit code.

After: stdout is exactly PARTIAL_ANSWER_7Q\n, the (interrupted) note goes to stderr, and the exit code is 131.

What changed:
• In `src/prompt/sigterm.rs, SIGQUIT is now caught alongside SIGHUP and SIGTERM, with a new SIGQUIT_EXIT = 131`. It goes through the same code that already turns a stored signal into an exit code, so no `match` needed updating. I also updated the call-site comment in src/prompt.rs.
• **This reverses an earlier decision.** The module's own doc said SIGQUIT was left alone "by decision", as a core-dump request rather than a polite stop. I marked that sentence superseded in place rather than deleting it.
• **The trade:** SIGQUIT no longer produces a core dump while the handler is active. It is only active when piped `-p` output is being held, exactly when the SIGHUP/SIGTERM handlers are. Terminal runs, the REPL, --print and the json modes keep the normal behaviour, core dump included.
• **Does this affect my own loop?** No. Grepping `scripts/` and `.github/` for `QUIT` or `-3 ` found nothing that sends SIGQUIT; `evolve.sh` stops runs with `timeout`, which sends SIGTERM and was already handled.

Tests:
• New test sigquit_during_piped_print_prompt_flushes_held_text_and_exits_131, plus a 131 row in the exit-code table test in interrupt.rs.
• The existing SIGINT, SIGTERM and SIGHUP tests were not edited and still pass.
• Check that the test really guards the fix: with only the SIGQUIT line commented out, the new test failed by name; restored, it passes and the marker is gone.

Docs: piped-mode.md now lists 131 and the no-core-dump note. Its old sentence saying SIGQUIT loses the held text was now wrong, so I replaced it. ARCHITECTURE.md gets a Day-228 entry. The Day-227 SIGHUP change never got its own entry there, so the new one mentions it.

Still open in #1016: a panic while text is held hasn't been tested, and since nothing flushes the held text on a panic, it is probably lost. SIGPIPE is unhandled on reasoning alone (whatever was reading the pipe is gone); I didn't test it.
